  "output": "## Reality check first\n\nBefore the category-by-category breakdown: this codebase is a **pre-implementation skeleton**, not a running system yet.\n\n- `engine/` — every pipeline stage (`normalize`, `blocking.block/score/combine`, `detect.classify/detect`, `agent/graph.py`), every eval module (`generate`, `baselines`, `harness`), and `db.py`'s writers are literal `raise NotImplementedError(...)` stubs. There is no query, no loop, no computation to profile.\n- `web/` — there is exactly **one** real DB call in the whole app (`listSources()` in `web/lib/server/sources.ts`), called once per page render, no loop. The only client components (`nav-rail`, `landing-hero`, `pipeline-strip`, `get-started-hero`, `step-list`, `sources-empty-state`) hold no state, run no effects, subscribe to nothing — they're `motion.div` wrappers with static entrance animations. There is nothing in the tree that re-renders more than once.\n\nSo for four of your five categories (N+1 queries, memory leaks, redundant computation, most caching) **there is currently nothing to find** — I'm not going to manufacture problems in code that doesn't exist. What I can give you, and what's actually useful given you're about to write this code (per `AGENTS.md`'s Phase 2–4 build order), is where each of these five failure modes will bite based on the exact routes/queries the spec already commits you to, with fixes sized to this project's stack.\n\n---\n\n## 1. N+1 queries — none today; two spots to pre-empt\n\n**`countSourcesBySide()`** (`web/lib/server/sources.ts:23`) is fine as-is — it filters an array already in memory, not a second query. No action needed.\n\n**Where it will happen:** the conflict queue (`GET /api/conflicts`, `AGENTS.md` §Web Route Handlers) needs the match, the two entities, and the source names for every conflict row shown in the diff. Written naively per-row, that's the textbook N+1:\n\n```ts\n// DON'T — one query per conflict row\nconst conflicts = await db.select().from(conflicts).where(...);\nfor (const c of conflicts) {\n  const match = await db.select().from(matches).where(eq(matches.id, c.matchId));\n  const entityA = await db.select().from(entities).where(eq(entities.id, match.entityAId));\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\n```ts\n// DO — one query with joins, matching the diff row's actual shape\nconst rows = await db\n  .select({\n    conflict: conflicts,\n    match: matches,\n    entityA: entitiesAlias(\"entity_a\"),\n    entityB: entitiesAlias(\"entity_b\"),\n  })\n  .from(conflicts)\n  .innerJoin(matches, eq(conflicts.matchId, matches.id))\n  .innerJoin(entityAAlias, eq(matches.entityAId, entityAAlias.id))\n  .innerJoin(entityBAlias, eq(matches.entityBId, entityBAlias.id))\n  .where(eq(conflicts.runId, runId))\n  .orderBy(desc(conflicts.severity));\n```\n\nSame shape applies to `engine/db.py`'s `write_entities`/`write_matches`/`write_conflicts` when you implement them — use `psycopg`'s `executemany`/`COPY` for the thousands-of-rows batch writes the run produces, not a per-row `INSERT`.\n\n## 2. React re-renders — nothing to fix; one thing to get right when it's built\n\nEvery current client component is stateless. The one place `AGENTS.md` calls out for actual client-side interactivity — the conflict queue's `j`/`k`/`a`/`e`/`o` keyboard nav and TanStack Query polling of run status — is exactly where re-render discipline matters:\n\n```tsx\n// When you build the conflict queue, keep row components memoized so a\n// polling status update to the run header doesn't re-render 400 diff rows.\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = memo(function ConflictDiffRow({ conflict }: { conflict: Conflict }) {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\n```tsx\n// TanStack Query poll — scope it to run status only, per AGENTS.md's\n// \"TanStack Query for run-status polling only\" rule. Don't let it re-fetch\n// or re-render the conflict list.\nconst { data: status } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: [\"run-status\", runId],\n  queryFn: () => fetch(`/api/runs/${runId}/status`).then((r) => r.json()),\n  refetchInterval: (query) =>\n    query.state.data?.status === \"complete\" ? false : 2000,\n});\n```\n\nThe `refetchInterval` returning `false` on terminal status matters — an infinite poll on a finished run is the most common leak in this exact pattern (see §4).\n\n## 3. Caching opportunities\n\nOne is already implemented correctly: `web/lib/db/index.ts` caches the Postgres connection on `globalThis` to survive Next.js HMR reloads in dev. Good.\n\nTwo are named explicitly in `AGENTS.md` and not yet wired up because there's no agent code yet:\n\n- **Prompt caching on the ruleset block** (`engine/agent/prompts.py`, currently a stub) — the ruleset is identical across every conflict in a run, so it must be the cached prefix, not re-sent per call. This is called out as \"the single largest cost lever\" — implement it when `prompts.py` is built, not retrofitted later.\n- **Batch API for `engine/eval/` sweeps** — the threshold sweep (`sweep_auto_apply_threshold` in `harness.py`) reprocesses the same manifest at 10 thresholds; route it through the Anthropic Batch API since eval runs are never latency-sensitive, per the spec.\n\n## 4. Memory leaks — none currently possible; the one real risk ahead\n\nNothing in the current tree holds a subscription, interval, or listener open — there's nothing to leak. The concrete risk is the run-status poll above: if `refetchInterval` doesn't check for terminal status (`complete`/`failed`), it polls forever after the component that cares is gone, and TanStack Query won't stop it for you unless the query is unmounted. The fix is the conditional `refetchInterval` shown above — build it in from the start rather than adding it after someone notices network tab noise.\n\nOn the engine side: `blocking.py`'s FAISS index (`TOP_K = 10` neighbours) will hold the full embedding index in memory for the duration of a run. That's expected, not a leak — just don't let `main.py`'s `execute_run` background task hold a reference to it past the run's completion.\n\n## 5. Redundant computation — nothing running yet; one thing the docstrings already protect against\n\nThere's no computation to be redundant. The one thing worth flagging positively: `blocking.py`'s own docstring already states the constraint that matters most here — \"Blocking exists so the pipeline is O(n·k), not O(n²). NEVER compare all pairs.\" That's the single biggest redundant-computation risk in a reconciliation engine (naive matching is quadratic), and the architecture already designed it out before writing the implementation. Just make sure `block()` actually does exact-key-first-then-embeddings when you implement it (per its docstring), not a shortcut that scores every pair.\n\nThe other likely spot: `pipeline/normalize.py`'s `fold_name()` will be called once per entity during normalization, then compared many times during matching. Don't re-fold on every comparison — fold once at normalize time (which is exactly what the `normalizedName` field on `CanonicalEntity` is for) and compare the pre-folded strings.\n\n---\n\n## Bottom line\n\nNothing to fix today. When you get to Phase 2 (`normalize` → `blocking` → `detect`) and Phase 4 (conflict queue), the two things worth building right the first time are: (1) join-based reads for the conflict queue instead of per-row queries, and (2) a `refetchInterval` on run-status polling that stops at terminal status. Everything else above is already anticipated correctly in the docstrings and `AGENTS.md` — the design just hasn't been implemented yet.\n",
